"""Original Bible-first study guides, with explicit primary passage references."""
ARTICLES=[]
def add(slug,title,summary,sections,passages,group='Accounts',extra_sources=()):
    ARTICLES.append(dict(slug='bible-'+slug,title=title,category='Faith & communities',summary=summary,sections=[dict(title=t,text=p) for t,p in sections],sources=['bible-webbe',*extra_sources],related=['ancient-assyria','nineveh'],note='Biblical primary-source guide. Read the cited passages in their full chapter context. Dates and identifications added by interpreters are distinguished from what the text itself says.',passages=passages,bible_group=group))

add('origins','Asshur, Nimrod and the earliest biblical geography','Assyria in Genesis, the Table of Nations and Balaam\'s oracles.',[
('Rivers and peoples','Genesis 2:14 places the Hiddekel in relation to Assyria and names the Euphrates as the fourth river. Genesis 10:8-12 introduces Nimrod and names Nineveh, Rehoboth Ir, Calah and Resen. Genesis 10:22 lists Asshur among the sons of Shem; 1 Chronicles 1:17 repeats that genealogy. These are the primary biblical starting points for Assyrian origins and geography.'),
('Read the names precisely','The retained translation reads Genesis 10:11 as movement into Assyria by the preceding subject. Other translations take Asshur as the subject. This grammatical question should be stated openly when discussing who built the named cities. Genesis 25:3 also names the Asshurim among Dedan\'s descendants; that occurrence should not automatically be equated with the Asshur of Genesis 10:22.'),
('Early references beyond Genesis','Genesis 25:18 uses Assyria as a geographical direction. Numbers 24:22-24 mentions Asshur in an oracle concerning captivity and affliction. These passages supply biblical language and relationships; converting them into a dated imperial boundary requires additional interpretation.')], [('GEN',2,14,14),('GEN',10,8,12),('GEN',10,22,22),('GEN',25,3,3),('GEN',25,18,18),('1CH',1,10,17),('NUM',24,21,24)],'Origins',('bible-genesis-translations',))

add('pul-menahem','Pul, Menahem and tribute to Assyria','The payment recorded in 2 Kings 15 and the deportation memory of 1 Chronicles 5.',[
('The account in Kings','2 Kings 15:19-20 records Pul, king of Assyria, coming against the land during Menahem\'s reign. Menahem gives him a thousand talents of silver to support his position as king. The payment is raised from wealthy men, at fifty shekels each. The narrative then says that the king of Assyria withdrew and did not remain in the land.'),
('The reference in Chronicles','1 Chronicles 5:26 names Pul and Tilgath Pilneser in its account of the captivity of the Reubenites, Gadites and half-tribe of Manasseh. Read that statement alongside 2 Kings rather than silently replacing the names with a modern spelling.'),
('Names and interpretation','Historical writing commonly identifies Pul with Tiglath-pileser III. That identification draws on comparison with other ancient evidence; 2 Kings 15:19 itself uses the name Pul. The archive preserves the name used in each passage and treats the historical identification as an additional explanatory step.')], [('2KI',15,19,20),('1CH',5,25,26)],extra_sources=('bible-pul-imj','aebp-tiglath'))

add('tiglath-pileser','Tiglath Pileser, Ahaz and the fall of Damascus','Campaigns, deportation and Judah\'s appeal to the Assyrian king.',[
('Israel and Damascus','2 Kings 15:29 names Tiglath Pileser as the king who captured northern Israelite places, including Galilee and the land of Naphtali, and carried people away to Assyria. In 2 Kings 16:7-9, Ahaz appeals to him against Syria and Israel, sends silver and gold, and describes himself as the Assyrian king\'s servant and son. Damascus is taken, its people are carried to Kir, and Rezin is killed.'),
('The altar and the court','2 Kings 16:10-18 continues beyond military events. Ahaz visits Damascus, sees an altar and sends its design to Urijah. Changes to worship and palace access are part of the biblical account of the relationship with Assyria.'),
('Read Chronicles alongside Kings','2 Chronicles 28:16-21 uses the spelling Tilgath Pilneser and stresses that the Assyrian intervention distressed Ahaz rather than strengthening him. The two accounts should be read together, retaining each book\'s emphasis instead of reducing the relationship to a list of victories.')], [('2KI',15,29,29),('2KI',16,7,18),('2CH',28,16,21)])

add('samaria','Shalmaneser, Hoshea and the fall of Samaria','The siege, captivity and explanation preserved in Kings.',[
('The siege narrative','2 Kings 17:3 names Shalmaneser as the Assyrian king to whom Hoshea became subject and paid tribute. The following verses describe Hoshea\'s approach to Egypt, the withholding of tribute, imprisonment and a three-year siege of Samaria. Verse 6 records the capture in Hoshea\'s ninth year and the removal of Israelites to Halah, the Habor at Gozan and cities of the Medes.'),
('The parallel chronological account','2 Kings 18:9-12 recounts the same siege using the regnal years of Hezekiah and Hoshea. It explicitly names Shalmaneser at the beginning and then speaks of the Assyrians taking the city. These are the biblical chronological statements; an absolute BCE date is a reconstruction from these and other evidence.'),
('The book\'s explanation','2 Kings 17:7-23 explains the catastrophe in terms of Israel\'s covenant conduct and the rejection of prophetic warnings. That theological interpretation is central to the biblical account. Later discussion of Sargon and the capture of Samaria should distinguish what Kings names from what other ancient records claim.')], [('2KI',17,1,23),('2KI',18,9,12)])

add('deportation','Deportation, resettlement and life under Assyrian rule','The places and communities named in Kings, Chronicles and Ezra.',[
('People carried away','2 Kings 17:6 and 18:11 name destinations for Israelites removed from Samaria. 1 Chronicles 5:6 singles out Beerah, while 5:25-26 recounts the removal of the eastern tribes to Halah, Habor, Hara and the river of Gozan. These references preserve more than a political conquest: they describe the displacement of communities.'),
('New inhabitants of Samaria','2 Kings 17:24-41 describes people brought from Babylon, Cuthah, Avva, Hamath and Sepharvaim into the cities of Samaria. The account includes the return of a priest and a description of mixed worship. Read the entire passage when using it to explain the religious situation portrayed by the biblical writer.'),
('Memory in Ezra','Ezra 4:2 recalls settlement under Esar Haddon; 4:9-10 lists communities and refers to Osnappar bringing people into Samaria and beyond the River. Osnappar\'s identification with a particular Assyrian ruler belongs to historical interpretation. The passage itself and the names it supplies remain the primary reference.')], [('2KI',17,6,6),('2KI',17,24,41),('2KI',18,11,11),('1CH',5,6,6),('1CH',5,25,26),('EZR',4,1,10)])

add('sargon','Sargon and the sign against Egypt and Cush','Isaiah 20 names an Assyrian king and the capture of Ashdod.',[
('The named king','Isaiah 20:1 refers to the year in which the Tartan came to Ashdod, sent by Sargon king of Assyria, and fought against it and took it. This short statement is the direct biblical reference to Sargon. The text calls the commander Tartan, an office title represented here as a name in the translation.'),
('The prophetic sign','Isaiah 20:2-4 describes Isaiah\'s enacted sign and says that the king of Assyria will lead captives from Egypt and exiles from Cush. The humiliation of those captives is an essential part of the oracle\'s warning.'),
('What the passage warns against','The chapter ends with the dismay of those who had relied on Egypt and Cush for help against Assyria. The chapter should therefore be read as both an account naming Ashdod and a prophetic warning about misplaced political confidence. It does not supply the full boundaries of Sargon\'s empire.')], [('ISA',20,1,6)],extra_sources=('bible-official-titles','aebp-sargon'))

add('sennacherib-invasion','Sennacherib invades Judah','The fortified cities, Lachish, tribute and the approach to Jerusalem.',[
('The invasion in Kings','2 Kings 18:13 records Sennacherib\'s attack on Judah\'s fortified cities in Hezekiah\'s fourteenth year. Verses 14-16 describe Hezekiah\'s payment of silver and gold, including gold removed from the doors and pillars of the temple. Lachish is the setting of the exchange with the Assyrian king.'),
('The delegation to Jerusalem','2 Kings 18:17-37 introduces the Tartan, Rab-saris and Rabshakeh, together with a large army. The speech challenges trust in Egypt, Hezekiah and the LORD. Isaiah 36 contains a parallel account, while 2 Chronicles 32:1-19 places the invasion alongside Hezekiah\'s defensive preparations and encouragement of the people.'),
('Read the connected chapters','This account continues into the prayer, prophetic response and deliverance in 2 Kings 19 and Isaiah 37. The archive keeps the political and religious dimensions together. Place names, the king\'s regnal year and the stated tribute should be cited directly to their passages before adding a modern chronology or archaeological comparison.')], [('2KI',18,13,37),('ISA',36,1,22),('2CH',32,1,19)],extra_sources=('oracc-rinap',))

add('rabshakeh','Rabshakeh, diplomacy and the language of Jerusalem','Officials, public speeches and the request to speak in Aramaic.',[
('Officials on both sides','2 Kings 18:17-18 names the Assyrian delegation and the Judean officials who meet it. Isaiah 36:2-3 gives a parallel description. The officials\' titles and names should be kept distinct: Rabshakeh and Tartan are conventional representations of court or military offices.'),
('Language as a political choice','In 2 Kings 18:26 and Isaiah 36:11, the Judean officials ask for Aramaic because they understand it, and ask that the speech not be made in the language of Judah within the hearing of the people on the wall. Rabshakeh then addresses that audience directly. The episode makes language part of the confrontation.'),
('The limits of the reference','These verses are important primary references for multilingual communication in the biblical account. They should not be turned into a claim that all ancient Assyrians used a single language, or that the language of this exchange was identical to a modern spoken Assyrian variety. Those are separate linguistic questions.')], [('2KI',18,17,37),('ISA',36,2,22)],'People & places',('bible-official-titles',))

add('hezekiah-deliverance','Hezekiah\'s prayer and Jerusalem\'s deliverance','The biblical account of prayer, Isaiah\'s response and the defeat of Sennacherib\'s army.',[
('Prayer before the LORD','2 Kings 19:14-19 describes Hezekiah receiving the letter, going to the house of the LORD, spreading it out and praying. He acknowledges Assyrian victories while distinguishing the living God from the destroyed idols of other nations. Isaiah 37:14-20 preserves the parallel prayer.'),
('The promised deliverance','The prophetic response in 2 Kings 19:20-34 addresses Assyrian boasting and promises that the king will not enter the city. Verses 35-37 record the angel of the LORD striking 185,000 in the Assyrian camp, Sennacherib\'s return to Nineveh and his later death. The number and the divine action are statements of the biblical narrative, cited here to their primary text.'),
('Parallel witness and later remembrance','Isaiah 37:21-38 records the same sequence, and 2 Chronicles 32:20-23 gives a shorter account. Sirach 48 and 2 Maccabees 8 and 15 later recall the deliverance. These connections help readers follow how the account was remembered within biblical literature.')], [('2KI',19,1,37),('ISA',37,1,38),('2CH',32,20,23)])

add('esarhaddon','Esar Haddon and the death of Sennacherib','Succession at Nineveh and a later reference to settlement in Samaria.',[
('The succession notice','2 Kings 19:36-37 says that Sennacherib returned to Nineveh and was killed by Adrammelech and Sharezer while worshipping in the house of Nisroch his god. The sons escaped into the land of Ararat, and Esar Haddon reigned in his place. Isaiah 37:37-38 preserves the parallel notice.'),
('Read the names in context','Adrammelech also appears in 2 Kings 17:31 as the name of a deity in the account of the settlers\' worship. Matching the spelling does not make the deity and Sennacherib\'s son the same subject. The verse and its context must accompany any name index.'),
('Esar Haddon in Ezra','Ezra 4:2 reports the speakers\' claim to have offered sacrifices since the time Esar Haddon brought them into the land. This is a later narrative preserving an Assyrian settlement memory. Other campaigns attributed to Esarhaddon, including his Egyptian conquests, require their own ancient sources; these particular biblical verses concern succession and settlement.')], [('2KI',19,36,37),('ISA',37,37,38),('EZR',4,1,5)])

add('manasseh','Manasseh taken by the commanders of Assyria','The captivity, repentance and restoration recorded in 2 Chronicles 33.',[
('Taken to Babylon','2 Chronicles 33:10-11 records that Manasseh and his people did not listen when the LORD spoke. Commanders of the army of the king of Assyria then take Manasseh, bind him with bronze fetters and carry him to Babylon. The destination in this passage is Babylon, even though the commanders serve the king of Assyria.'),
('Prayer and return','Verses 12-13 describe Manasseh humbling himself, praying and being brought back to Jerusalem and his kingdom. The narrative continues with defensive works and changes in worship. Its focus includes repentance and restoration as well as captivity.'),
('A king left unnamed','The passage does not name the Assyrian king responsible. Assigning the event to Esarhaddon or Ashurbanipal is a proposed historical identification, not a name supplied by 2 Chronicles 33. The Prayer of Manasses, included among this edition\'s additional books, is a related devotional text and should be identified separately from the account in Chronicles.')], [('2CH',33,10,20),('PRM',1,1,15)])

add('jonah','Jonah, Nineveh and God\'s compassion','The mission, repentance and final question at the heart of the book of Jonah.',[
('Called to Nineveh','Jonah 1:1-2 introduces the call to go to Nineveh because its wickedness has come before the LORD. The opening flight, storm and rescue lead to a renewed commission in chapter 3. The narrative\'s focus is the prophet\'s response as well as the city\'s.'),
('The response of the city','Jonah 3 records the proclamation of impending overthrow, the people\'s belief, fasting and the king\'s decree. The king is called the king of Nineveh; the text does not supply his personal name. God sees their turning from evil and relents from the threatened disaster.'),
('Compassion and the prophet','Chapter 4 records Jonah\'s anger and God\'s question concerning the city, its people and its animals. Read this chapter with chapter 3 so that Nineveh is encountered through the book\'s account of mercy as well as judgement. Matthew 12:41 and Luke 11:32 later refer to the Ninevites\' repentance. Second Kings 14:25 separately names Jonah son of Amittai in an account of Israel\'s restored border.')], [('JON',1,1,17),('JON',2,1,10),('JON',3,1,10),('JON',4,1,11),('2KI',14,25,25)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('nahum','Nahum and the oracle concerning Nineveh','The prophetic book\'s language of judgement, violence and the fall of a city.',[
('The subject named at the start','Nahum 1:1 identifies the work as an oracle about Nineveh and a vision of Nahum the Elkoshite. The first chapter speaks of the LORD\'s power, justice and protection. Its opening should remain attached to the later descriptions of the city\'s fall.'),
('The city under assault','Nahum 2 uses vivid imagery of defence, attack, plunder and a lion\'s dwelling. Nahum 3 addresses a city associated with bloodshed, deceit and exploitation. It compares its security with that of No-Amon and ends with a direct address to the king of Assyria.'),
('Read alongside Jonah','Jonah and Nahum both concern Nineveh, but they have different narrative and prophetic settings. Their pairing should preserve each book\'s message: repentance and compassion in Jonah; judgement against violence and oppression in Nahum. The book of Nahum does not itself give a BCE date for Nineveh\'s fall; such a date belongs to comparison with other historical evidence.')], [('NAH',1,1,15),('NAH',2,1,13),('NAH',3,1,19)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('isaiah-assyria','Assyria in Isaiah: power, judgement and trust','Isaiah\'s account of Assyria as an instrument of judgement whose pride is also judged.',[
('The Assyrian threat','Isaiah 7:17-20 names Assyria in a warning to Ahaz. Isaiah 8:5-8 compares the king of Assyria and his glory with the river\'s mighty floodwaters. The imagery presents a political threat through a prophetic interpretation of events.'),
('The rod and the boasting ruler','Isaiah 10:5-19 calls Assyria the rod of God\'s anger, then addresses the ruler\'s destructive intention and pride. The chapter distinguishes being used for judgement from possessing independent or unlimited authority. Isaiah 14:24-27 announces the breaking of the Assyrian in God\'s land.'),
('Trust and deliverance','Isaiah 30:27-33 and 31:1-9 join warnings about reliance on Egypt to assurances concerning Assyria. Isaiah 36-37 supplies the narrative of Hezekiah, the Assyrian challenge and Jerusalem\'s deliverance. These chapters should be read with Isaiah\'s hopeful references to Assyria, especially chapter 19, rather than selecting only passages of conflict.')], [('ISA',7,17,20),('ISA',8,5,8),('ISA',10,5,34),('ISA',14,24,27),('ISA',30,27,33),('ISA',31,1,9)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('isaiah-blessing','Assyria, the work of God\'s hands','Isaiah 19:23-25 and the vision of blessing shared with Egypt and Israel.',[
('The highway and shared worship','Isaiah 19:23 speaks of a highway from Egypt to Assyria, movement between the two lands and shared worship. Verse 24 places Israel with Egypt and Assyria as a blessing in the midst of the earth. The passage moves beyond the hostility described elsewhere in the chapter.'),
('The blessing itself','Isaiah 19:25 names Egypt as God\'s people, Assyria as the work of his hands and Israel as his inheritance. The full verse is retained locally and should be read with verses 23-24. It is a central biblical passage for this archive\'s treatment of Assyria and hope.'),
('Return and gathering','Isaiah 11:11-16 includes Assyria among places from which a remnant is gathered, while Isaiah 27:12-13 speaks of those ready to perish in Assyria coming to worship in Jerusalem. These passages support a reading route through restoration and worship. Interpretations of how and when these prophecies are fulfilled should be identified as interpretations, with their scriptural reasoning stated.')], [('ISA',19,23,25),('ISA',11,11,16),('ISA',27,12,13)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('hosea','Assyria in Hosea: alliances, exile and return','The prophetic critique of seeking security in Assyria and the promises of restoration.',[
('Turning to foreign powers','Hosea 5:13 portrays Ephraim going to Assyria for help that cannot heal. Hosea 7:11 compares Ephraim with a senseless dove calling to Egypt and going to Assyria. Hosea 8:8-10 and 12:1 continue the criticism of foreign entanglements and treaties.'),
('Captivity and judgement','Hosea 9:3 and 11:5 speak of Assyria in relation to exile and rule. Hosea 10:6 concerns the removal of an object of worship as tribute. Verse 14 mentions Shalman and Beth Arbel; the personal identification and location are not fully explained by that verse and should not be presented as settled by its wording alone.'),
('A different final confidence','Hosea 11:10-11 imagines a return from Egypt and Assyria. Hosea 14:3 says that Assyria will not save, setting political rescue against the appeal for mercy. The book\'s Assyrian references therefore belong to its connected account of judgement, repentance and restoration, rather than to a list of wars alone.')], [('HOS',5,13,13),('HOS',7,11,13),('HOS',8,8,10),('HOS',9,3,3),('HOS',10,6,14),('HOS',11,5,11),('HOS',12,1,1),('HOS',14,1,4)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('ezekiel','Assyria in Ezekiel: alliances and the great cedar','The imagery of Ezekiel 23, 31 and 32.',[
('The attraction of Assyrian power','Ezekiel 23 portrays Samaria and Jerusalem through the figures of Oholah and Oholibah. Assyrian officers, clothing, horses and political relationships form part of the imagery. The chapter uses disturbing sexual and violent language to express its prophetic judgement; that literary form should be identified when quoting individual verses.'),
('The cedar in chapter 31','Ezekiel 31 addresses Pharaoh and uses Assyria as the image of a magnificent cedar, nourished by waters and sheltering many nations. Its height and pride lead into the account of its fall. The tree is a prophetic comparison, not a botanical description of the extent of the empire.'),
('Among the fallen','Ezekiel 32:22-23 places Assyria and its company among the slain. Read the image within the surrounding lament over the nations and the ruler being addressed. Together these passages use Assyrian power and collapse to communicate a warning about human pride and the limits of political greatness.')], [('EZE',23,1,49),('EZE',31,1,18),('EZE',32,22,23)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('micah','Assyria and the land of Nimrod in Micah','Threat, deliverance and gathering in Micah 5 and 7.',[
('The ruler and the Assyrian','Micah 5:2-6 moves from the promised ruler associated with Bethlehem to peace and defence when the Assyrian enters the land. Verse 6 places the land of Assyria alongside the land of Nimrod. Keep this wording with its prophetic setting when connecting the passage to Genesis 10.'),
('A vision of coming from many lands','Micah 7:12 includes Assyria and the cities of Egypt in its picture of people coming. The chapter continues through desolation, shepherding, the nations\' response and the praise of God\'s mercy. The Assyrian reference is part of that wider movement.'),
('Prophecy and chronology','Micah\'s language can be discussed in relation to an Assyrian historical setting and in theological readings of the promised ruler. An interpretation should say which connection it is making. The text\'s named places do not, by themselves, establish a precise boundary or a modern territorial entitlement.')], [('MIC',5,2,6),('MIC',7,12,20)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('zephaniah','Zephaniah and Nineveh\'s desolation','The judgement oracle in Zephaniah 2:13-15.',[
('Assyria and Nineveh named together','Zephaniah 2:13 announces the destruction of Assyria and the desolation of Nineveh. Verses 14-15 picture animals among the ruins and the astonishment of those passing by. The city is described through its former sense of security and self-sufficiency.'),
('The surrounding nations','This short oracle belongs to a chapter addressing several peoples and places. Reading the whole chapter prevents the Assyrian passage from being detached from the book\'s wider warning and call to seek the LORD.'),
('What is supplied by the text','The passage is a primary biblical witness to the announcement of Nineveh\'s desolation. It does not name the armies that captured the city or give an absolute year. Those details should be supplied from their own sources when a historical article compares prophecy with the surviving accounts of the city\'s fall.')], [('ZEP',2,1,15)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('other-prophets','Assyria in prophetic warnings, lament and prayer','Jeremiah, Lamentations, Psalms, Nehemiah, Amos and Zechariah.',[
('Foreign dependence and oppression','Jeremiah 2:18 asks about going to Egypt and Assyria for water; 2:36 warns that confidence in both will bring shame. Jeremiah 50:17-20 recalls Israel as scattered sheep, with the king of Assyria as one of the powers that consumed them. Lamentations 5:6 names dependence on Egypt and Assyria for bread. Isaiah 52:4 recalls Assyrian oppression.'),
('Prayer and historical memory','Psalm 83:8 names Assyria in the company of hostile peoples. Nehemiah 9:32 recalls affliction from the days of the kings of Assyria onward. These passages preserve the place of Assyria within Israel\'s memory and prayer, without each supplying a separate campaign narrative.'),
('Exile and gathering','Amos 5:27 announces exile beyond Damascus and 6:14 announces an unnamed oppressing nation; connecting that nation specifically with Assyria is a contextual historical interpretation. Zechariah 10:10-11 explicitly names gathering from Assyria and the bringing down of its pride. Reading these passages together reveals both the memory of oppression and the language of restoration.')], [('JER',2,18,18),('JER',2,36,36),('JER',50,17,20),('LAM',5,6,6),('ISA',52,4,4),('PSA',83,1,18),('NEH',9,32,32),('AMO',5,27,27),('AMO',6,14,14),('ZEC',10,10,11)],'Prophecy & hope')

add('ezra-memory','The king of Assyria in Ezra\'s later setting','Assyrian settlement memories and the wording of Ezra 6:22.',[
('The settlement claim in chapter 4','Ezra 4:2 refers to Esar Haddon bringing people into the land. Verses 9-10 name additional groups and refer to Osnappar. These statements belong to the book\'s account of opposition to rebuilding, and preserve claims about the origins of settled populations.'),
('The wording in chapter 6','Ezra 6:22 says that the LORD had turned the heart of the king of Assyria towards the returned community to strengthen the work on the temple. The chapter\'s wider setting names Darius and recalls earlier royal decrees. Both the title in verse 22 and that surrounding setting should remain visible.'),
('A title is not a new imperial timeline','Explanations of why the title king of Assyria is used here are interpretive questions. The wording should be preserved and investigated, rather than silently changed. It should also not be used alone to extend the same seventh-century Assyrian state through every later ruler who held authority in the region. First Esdras 7:15 preserves a related reference in the additional books.')], [('EZR',4,1,10),('EZR',6,1,22),('1ES',7,15,15)],'Later remembrance')

add('new-testament','The Ninevites in the words of Jesus','Matthew 12 and Luke 11 recall Jonah and the repentance of Nineveh.',[
('Matthew\'s account','Matthew 12:38-42 records the request for a sign, Jesus\' response concerning Jonah and the reference to the men of Nineveh. Verse 41 says they will rise in judgement because they repented at Jonah\'s preaching, while someone greater than Jonah is present. The Queen of the South appears in the following comparison.'),
('Luke\'s account','Luke 11:29-32 similarly speaks of Jonah as a sign to the Ninevites and of their repentance. Reading Matthew and Luke alongside Jonah 3 keeps the connection attached to the passages that make it.'),
('An important distinction','These New Testament references concern Nineveh\'s response in the biblical account. They are not New Testament descriptions of the boundaries of the ancient empire or a census of a later Assyrian community. Acts 2:9 also names Mesopotamia among the origins of those hearing the apostles, but does not explicitly identify that entire group as Assyrians.')], [('MAT',12,38,42),('LUK',11,29,32),('JON',3,1,10),('ACT',2,5,11)],'New Testament')

add('tobit','Tobit: exile, Nineveh and faithfulness','An account of a displaced household under Assyrian rulers, with all fourteen chapters available locally.',[
('The Assyrian setting','Tobit 1 places Tobit\'s captivity in the days of Enemessar king of the Assyrians and describes his life at Nineveh. The retained translation later names Sennacherib and Sarchedonus. These spellings should be retained when discussing this edition; other textual traditions and translations can use different forms.'),
('Life in exile','The opening chapters emphasise almsgiving, food, burial of the dead and the dangers of loyalty under royal power. Tobit\'s position changes as rulers change, while his household becomes the setting for the book\'s connected story of suffering, prayer, travel and restoration.'),
('Nineveh in the closing chapter','Tobit 14 urges departure from Nineveh and recalls its anticipated destruction. The verse wording of this retained edition should be compared carefully when another translation refers to a different prophet or gives different details. Tobit is included in Catholic and many Orthodox Bibles and is treated differently in other traditions. Inclusion here makes its Assyrian material accessible without imposing one canon on every reader.')], [('TOB', 1, 1, 22), ('TOB', 2, 1, 14), ('TOB', 3, 1, 17), ('TOB', 4, 1, 21), ('TOB', 5, 1, 22), ('TOB', 6, 1, 17), ('TOB', 7, 1, 18), ('TOB', 8, 1, 21), ('TOB', 9, 1, 6), ('TOB', 10, 1, 12), ('TOB', 11, 1, 19), ('TOB', 12, 1, 22), ('TOB', 13, 1, 18), ('TOB', 14, 1, 15)],'Deuterocanon & additional books')

add('judith','Judith, Holofernes and the Assyrian army','The biblical narrative, its named rulers and the question of historical setting.',[
('Read the narrative as it is written','Judith 1:1 calls Nebuchadnezzar the ruler of the Assyrians in Nineveh. Chapters 1-3 describe royal war, demands for submission and Holofernes\' mission. The account then moves to the resistance of the Judean community, the siege and Judith\'s intervention. The full sixteen chapters are available in the local reader.'),
('Judith\'s action and the deliverance','The narrative follows Judith\'s prayer, entry into the enemy camp, killing of Holofernes and return. Chapters 14-16 describe the rout and celebration. Its concerns include trust in God, courage and the defeat of overwhelming force.'),
('The historical question stays visible','The identification of Nebuchadnezzar as an Assyrian ruler at Nineveh differs from the conventional historical chronology. The USCCB introduction interprets the book\'s combined settings as historical fiction with a theological purpose. That is an identified interpretation, not a replacement of Judith\'s wording. The archive retains the biblical account and distinguishes it from a reconstructed imperial chronology. Canonical status also varies between Christian traditions.')], [('JDT',c,1,n) for c,n in [(1,16),(2,28),(3,10),(4,15),(5,24),(6,21),(7,32),(8,36),(9,14),(10,23),(11,23),(12,20),(13,20),(14,19),(15,13),(16,25)]],'Deuterocanon & additional books',('bible-judith-usccb',))

add('later-remembrance','Assyria remembered in the additional books','Sirach, Maccabees and Esdras preserve further Assyrian references.',[
('Remembering Hezekiah and Sennacherib','Sirach 48:17-22 recalls Hezekiah, Isaiah, Sennacherib and the defeat of the Assyrian camp. Second Maccabees 8:19 and 15:22 appeal to the same deliverance in later situations of danger; 3 Maccabees 6:5 also invokes the defeat of Sennacherim. These are valuable examples of biblical remembrance and prayer.'),
('Assyria in other literary settings','First Esdras 5:69 and 7:15 preserve references to settlement and the king of Assyria. Second Esdras 13:39-47 describes the ten tribes taken away by Salmananser in an apocalyptic vision. Fourth Maccabees 13:9 refers to the three young men in Assyria. Each reference should be read in its own book and genre.'),
('Edition and canon','These books do not all share the same status in Jewish, Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant traditions. The retained Bible edition includes them in an extended collection, and the site labels that collection explicitly. The source code 4ES is displayed here as 2 Esdras, following the retained edition\'s book title; different numbering systems can otherwise cause confusion.')], [('SIR',48,17,22),('2MA',8,19,19),('2MA',15,22,22),('3MA',6,5,5),('1ES',5,69,69),('1ES',7,15,15),('4ES',13,39,47),('4MA',13,9,9)],'Deuterocanon & additional books')

add('reading-guide','How to read the biblical references','Primary text, translation, chronology and transparent interpretation.',[
('Start with the passage','For events recorded in Scripture, this archive begins with the biblical account and identifies the book, chapter and verse. Follow the local reference links to the complete chapter. Parallel accounts are shown together so readers can compare what each book records.'),
('Keep the textual layers visible','The biblical wording, the choice of translation and an interpreter\'s explanation are related but distinct. Genesis 10:11 raises a question about its subject; Hosea 10:14 leaves Shalman\'s precise identity unstated; Ezra 6:22 uses an Assyrian royal title in a later setting. The site preserves the wording while identifying those questions.'),
('Use other evidence with a clear purpose','Archaeology, inscriptions and historical scholarship can supply context, proposed identifications and absolute dates. They are cited separately, so that a reader can tell when a claim comes directly from Scripture and when it comes from comparison or reconstruction. The Bible-first approach does not require presenting every proposed harmonisation as something the text itself explicitly states.'),
('Scope of the verse index','The index is a reproducible search of the retained translation for the published names and aliases. It includes ambiguous name matches with context and separately labelled additional books. The topic guides add related passages that do not use a search term. This provides extensive coverage without claiming to settle every indirect allusion, translation variant or theological reading.')], [('GEN',10,11,11),('HOS',10,14,14),('EZR',6,22,22)],'Reading & interpretation')

